      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "In Utah, an asphalt shingle roof typically costs $8,500 to $18,000 installed, while a metal roof ranges from $15,000 to $48,000 depending on the specific metal system.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "an asphalt shingle roof typically costs $8,500 to $18,000 installed, while a metal roof ranges from $15,000 to $48,000"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Upfront Cost Breakdown per Square Foot"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles: $4.50 to $8.00 per square foot ($10,000 \u2013 $18,000 total for a standard home)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Class 4 Impact-Resistant Asphalt: $14,000 to $22,000 total."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Exposed Fastener / Metal Panels: Lower-cost metal option, often $5.00 to $9.00 per square foot."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Standing Seam Metal: $9.00 to $16.00+ per square foot ($25,000 \u2013 $48,000 for a 2,500 sq. ft. footprint with 24-gauge material)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Lifespan & Long-Term Value"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt: Lasts 20 to 30 years. You will likely need to replace an asphalt roof two or three times over a 50-to-60-year span."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal: Lasts 40 to 70 years. While the initial price is roughly double or triple that of asphalt, it usually only requires a single installation over its lifespan."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Utah Weather Performance"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Snow: Metal roofs naturally shed heavy mountain snow much faster than asphalt."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Sun & Wind: High-altitude UV rays and intense summer heat degrade asphalt faster, whereas metal reflects heat well and handles high winds reliably if fastened properly."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
